---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import cover from '../../public/zhangleebook.png';
---

<Layout>
	<div class="mx-6 grid gap-12 lg:mx-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(280px,420px)_minmax(0,720px)] lg:items-start lg:justify-center">
		<div class="flex justify-center">
			<Image class="w-72 rounded-2xl border border-slate-800 shadow-2xl lg:w-[24rem]" src={cover} alt="Front cover of the book Data Management Interactions with Computer Architecture and Systems"/>
		</div>
		<section class="text-slate-200">
			<div class="rounded-3xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/70 p-8 shadow-xl">
				<p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-sky-300">Cambridge University Press</p>
				<h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl text-amber-300 lg:text-6xl">Data Management</h1>
				<h2 class="mt-3 text-2xl text-slate-100 lg:text-4xl">Interactions with Computer Architecture and Systems</h2>
				<p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
					This book illuminates the intricate relationship between data management, computer architecture,
					and system software. It traces the evolution of computing to today&apos;s data-centric focus and
					shows why hardware-software codesign is essential for building data processing systems with
					high throughput and low latency.
				</p>
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
					It covers logical data formats, memory architecture, GPU programming, and the innovative use of
					ray tracing in computation, with special emphasis on minimizing data movement and optimizing
					data storage and retrieval. The book is written for professionals and students seeking both
					fundamental principles and practical system insights.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
					<a class="rounded-xl bg-amber-300 px-5 py-3 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-950 transition hover:bg-amber-200" href="https://www.amazon.com/Data-Management-Interactions-Computer-Architecture/dp/1009123319/" target="_blank">
						Available on Amazon
					</a>
					<a class="rounded-xl border border-slate-700 px-5 py-3 text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 transition hover:border-slate-500 hover:bg-slate-800" href="/errata">
						View Errata
					</a>
				</div>

				<div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl text-slate-100">Authors</h2>
						<div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-xl text-amber-300">Xiaodong Zhang</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-slate-300">
									Robert M. Critchfield Professor in Engineering and University Distinguished Scholar
									at The Ohio State University. He specializes in data management in computer and
									distributed systems, and his influential research has been broadly adopted in practice.
								</p>
							</div>
							<div>
								<h3 class="text-xl text-amber-300">Rubao Lee</h3>
								<p class="mt-1 text-slate-300">
									Computer scientist whose work has made significant contributions to GPU-accelerated
									database systems and data processing. His innovations, including RCFile and YSmart,
									have been widely adopted in industry.
								</p>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
					<div>
						<h2 class="text-2xl text-slate-100">Who This Book Is For</h2>
						<p class="mt-4 text-slate-300">
							The book is written for senior undergraduates and beginning graduate students who have
							completed entry-level courses in computer architecture, operating systems, and databases.
							Its goal is to break down the classroom barriers among data, architecture, and systems.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-8 rounded-3xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/70 p-8 shadow-xl">
				<h2 class="text-3xl text-slate-100">Contents at a Glance</h2>
				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div>
						<ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">1.</span> Introduction</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">2.</span> Data Storage: Physical Allocation and Logical Format</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">3.</span> Main Memory: The Physical Space</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">4.</span> Buffer Replacement Algorithms</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">5.</span> In-Memory Data Processing in Large Data Centers</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div>
						<ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-300">
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">6.</span> GPU Computing: A New Algorithm-to-Architecture Interaction</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">7.</span> GPU for Structured Data</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">8.</span> GPU for Spatial Data</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">9.</span> Ray Tracing Hardware in GPUs for Accelerated Computation</li>
							<li><span class="font-semibold text-slate-100">10.</span> The Future of Computing: Synergies in Data Management and System Architecture</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-8 rounded-3xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/70 p-8 shadow-xl">
				<h2 class="text-3xl text-slate-100">From the Preface</h2>
				<div class="mt-6 space-y-5 text-slate-300">
					<p>
						Computing has evolved from scientific number crunching, to networking, and now to
						data-centric computing. In this new era, processing and analyzing massive volumes of data
						has become central to both system design and application performance.
					</p>
					<p>
						The book argues that applications, system software, and hardware architecture must be
						understood together. In the post-Moore&apos;s-Law era, efficient execution depends on balancing
						low algorithmic complexity, minimal data movement, and high parallelism.
					</p>
					<p>
						Its central teaching goal is to break down subject boundaries and give readers an interactive
						view of data, architecture, and systems, so they can identify the practical performance
						sweet spots across the computing stack.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>
